#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054554 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e77fe2f6-0e93-5378-aa8d-cc6d5122bdaa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Rational Function With Common Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "f4a45939-a80c-5e3e-bf2f-b3097a42c2ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-5137" :question/problem "Solve $f(x)=0$, where $f(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{x^2-x-2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x + 1}{x^{2} - x - 2} \\\\\n&= (x + 1)/((x + 1)(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 1))/((x + 1)(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the numerator equal to zero, we get $1=0$, which is a false statement. Therefore,\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nhas no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d98f7bd-2351-5eb8-80ff-67adcba4adbc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Rational Function With a Quadratic Numerator and No Common Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "bf127b16-01c5-58c9-903a-e00fe349c011" :question/math-academy-id "e-5139" :question/problem "Solve $f(x)=0$, where $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-4x}{x^2+5x+6}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} - 4x}{x^{2} + 5x + 6} \\\\\n&= (x(x - 4))/((x + 2)(x + 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nare $x=0$ and $x=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054556 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "688b4f72-387b-5757-9b81-d24a1d9c727d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Rational Function Containing a Cubic Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "6d6b343f-756f-5d3b-9231-fe7796d85653" :question/math-academy-id "e-5142" :question/problem "Solve $f(x)=0$, where $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-9x+18}{x^2-x-2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{3} - 2x^{2} - 9x + 18}{x^{2} - x - 2} \\\\\n&= (x^{2}(x - 2) - 9(x - 2))/((x - 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x^{2} - 9))/((x - 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x - 3)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x - 3)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 3)(x + 3))/(x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the numerator equal to zero and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+3) = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\pm3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nare $x=3$ and $x=-3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054566 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d084b4e-7ca3-5569-b2a5-42c4b8f47917" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Rational Function With a Linear Numerator and No Common Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "b8371b7c-dfd8-52b3-9c34-00d6814cc5de" :question/math-academy-id "e-5257" :question/problem "Solve $f(x)=0$, where $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x + 1}{x^2 + x - 6}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2x + 1}{x^{2} + x - 6} \\\\\n&= (2x + 1)/((x + 3)(x - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2x &= -1 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"}]}
